5:## Understanding the heterogeneity of dynamic functional connectivity patterns in first-episode drug naïve depression using normative models
25:Keywords: Normative model Major depressive disorder fMRI Dynamic functional connectivity
29:Quantitative biomarkers have been used in many branches of medicine to aid diagnosis and treatment (Kalia, 2015; Woong-Shick et al., 2005). However, mental disorders are diagnosed exclusively based on
33:Background: The heterogeneity of the clinical symptoms and presumptive neural pathologies has stunted progress toward  identifying  reproducible  biomarkers  and  limited  therapeutic  interventions'  effectiveness  for  the  first episode drug-naïve major depressive disorders (FEDN-MDD). This study combined the dynamic features of fMRI data and normative modeling to quantitative and individualized metrics for delineating the biological heterogeneity of FEDN-MDD.
35:Method: Two hundred seventy-four adults with FEDN-MDD and 832 healthy controls from International Big-Data Center for Depression Research were included. Subject-specific dynamic brain networks and network fluctuation characteristics  were  computed  for  each  subject  using  the  group  information-guided  independent  component analysis. Then, we mapped the heterogeneity of the dynamic features (network fluctuation characteristics and dynamic functional connectivity within brain networks) in the patients group via normative modeling.
37:Results: The  FEDN-MDD  whose  network  fluctuation  characteristics  deviate  from  the  normative  model  also showed significant differences within the default mode network, executive control network, and limbic network compared with healthy controls. Furthermore, the network fluctuation characteristics are significantly increased in patients with FEDN-MDD. About 4.74 % of the patients showed a deviation of dynamic functional connectivity, and only 3.35 % of the controls deviated from the normative model in above 100 connectivities. More patients than healthy controls showed extreme dynamic variabilities in above 100 connectivities.
39:Conclusions: This work evaluates the efficacy of an individualized approach based on normative modeling for understanding the heterogeneity of abnormal dynamic functional connectivity patterns in FEDN-MDD, and could be used as complementary to classical case-control comparisons.
41:the  symptoms  reported  by  patients  and  their  relatives,  and  the  biomarkers for mental disorders are still to be developed (Abi-Dargham and Horga, 2016). As one of the most common and costly psychiatric disorders in the world, depression (major depressive disorder [MDD] or clinical depression) has been studied extensively. However, the precise
65:etiology and underlying mechanisms of MDD remain elusive, and the identification  of  reliable  biomarkers  has  been  hindered  due  to  its extreme  heterogeneity.  According  to  the  Diagnostic  and  Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (5th edition) (DSM-5), MDD is characterized by clinically manifesting at least five symptoms out of nine, and these different symptom combinations lead to the high heterogeneity of clinical  presentations  among  patients.  Profiling  the  individual  differences in patients infected with the same disease would be helpful for the understanding of the underlying neuropathology and the development of precision medicine.
67:Although abundant literature exists with regard to functional brain network  abnormalities  in  MDD,  only  a  very  few  robust  results  are replicative.  The  divergent  results  are  mainly  due  to  the  clinical complexity  of  MDD  together  with  the  differences  in  medication  and study protocols. In most fMRI studies, functional connectivity is typically assumed to be stable during the entire scan. There is an increasing interest in examining the dynamic changes in functional connectivity (Zhang et al., 2021), which could lead to the identification of specific dynamic connectivity alterations and explain the inconsistency in previous functional connectivity. However, it is unclear to what extent the inconsistently are attributed to the heterogeneity of the disease, or the variety of methods and measurements. The case-control paradigm assumes that the whole MDD patients are affected by the disease to the same  extent  and  are  uniform,  homogeneous  entities.  However,  this assumption is problematic because MDD is mainly diagnosed based on self-reported heterogeneous symptom combinations, and different participants may have different symptom profiles and different etiological pathways, and resulting neurobiological changes may converge on the same symptoms (Alexander-Bloch et al., 2014; Marquand et al., 2019). Therefore, to understand the neurobiology of MDD, it is important to understand  the  range  of  associated  neurobiological  variation,  which may subsequently inform intervention at the level of the individual in the spirit of ' precision medicine ' . Over the years, many different theoretical models have been proposed to explain the heterogeneity of psychiatric disorders. A normative model can be understood as a statistical model that maps demographic or behavioral variables to a quantitative brain readout, and we can consider the abnormal characteristics of each patient  as  an  extreme  value  within  this  distribution.  The  normative model can be used to fractionate heterogeneous clinical groups and to find outliers within the heterogeneous cohort. The normative model has been used in schizophrenia, bipolar, and autism spectrum disorders to map the variation within the patient's cohort and address heterogeneity by fractionating cohorts (Baldwin et al., 2022; Bethlehem et al., 2020; Chien et al., 2022; Marquand et al., 2016; Wolfers et al., 2018; Zabihi et al., 2019). Previous studies also confirmed that deviations from the normative  model  were  frequent  in  psychiatric  disorders  but  highly heterogeneous. In the present study, we used the normative modeling approach  that  maps  interindividual  deviations  in  patients  with  firstepisode  drug  naïve  depression  (FEDN-MDD)  in  reference  to  the healthy  range  (see  Fig.  1,  an  overview  of  the  proposed  normative modeling approach showing the steps in the pipeline). Firstly, a slidingtime  window  method  was  used  to  compute  the  dynamic  functional connectivity  matrices  for  each  participant.  Secondly,  subject-specific inherent connectivity networks and corresponding time-varying weight  variability  were  computed  for  each  subject  using  a  group information-guided  independent component analysis technique (GIG-
69:Fig. 1. Data analysis flowchart. (A) Resting-state fMRI data were collected to create dynamic functional connectivity matrices based on AAL atlas using a sliding time window method. (B) Healthy-ICA controls were used to compute the subject-specific networks at the group level, and then these group-level networks were used as guidance information. (C) The back reconstruction algorithm of GIG-ICA was used to compute networks with the time-varying weight of the remaining participants (FEDN-MDD, healthy-base and healthy-comparison individuals), and then the FC-dynamic and network-dynamic characteristics were calculated for each individual. (D) For the characteristics of healthy-base controls, the normative models were estimated between any characteristics and age to identify the normative distribution. (E)  The deviations of each FEDN-MDD and healthy-comparison individual were measured based on the normative models from step (D). (F) Comparison was performed on deviations across groups.
75:ICA). Then, the FC-dynamic (dynamic of functional connectivity) characteristics and network fluctuation characteristics were calculated based on these connectivity networks. Thirdly, quantile polynomial regression was used to build the normative model based on age and each dynamic characteristic in a large healthy cohort to identify the normative range. Finally, we positioned each patient with FEDN-MDD and healthy participants within the normative ranges to estimate the deviation in individual  cases  regarding  these  dynamic  characteristics.  Through  this approach, we expected to 1) map the dynamic characteristics by which each FEDN-MDD participant differs from the expected functional connectivity patterns, and thereby; 2) demonstrate the value of normative modeling techniques for understanding the biological heterogeneity of MDD. The present study will provide a quantitative estimate of the degree of heterogeneity, in terms of the dynamic features (network fluctuation characteristics and FC-dynamic  characteristic) deviations, related to FEDN-MDD.
81:We employed data from International Big-Data Center for Depression Research,  and  the  data  were  collected  from  multiple  hospitals/institutions in China (http://yanlab.psych.ac.cn/IBCDR). Full details on the datasets and preprocessing pipeline have been described previously (Yan et al., 2019). According to the information provided by IBCDR, we selected  subjects  for  group  statistical  analyses  from  1276  MDDs  and 1104  healthy  controls  through  the  following  criteria:  1)  FEDN-MDD aged from 18 to 50 were included, resulting in 276 FEDN-MDDs; 2) healthy controls with age in a range of 18 to 50 were included, resulting in 847 controls; 3) subjects with excessive head motion (mean FD &gt; 0.5 mm or max FD &gt; 2 mm) were excluded, resulting in 274 FEDN-MDDs and 832 controls; 4) 125 healthy control individuals (71 females, as a healthy-ICA group) of 832 controls were included to create the group level  components using GIG-ICA; 5) Another 289 healthy control individuals (165 female, as a healthy-base group) of 832 controls were included to establish a normative model; 6) 274 FEDN-MDD individuals (174 males) and the rest 418 healthy control individuals (232 males, as a healthy-comparison group) recruited from the same study sites were included to conduct statistical analyses. We divided the healthy controls into  three  groups  to  avoid  reusing  data  in  different  steps  of  analysis which might lead to biased results. There were no significant differences between the depression and healthy comparison control cohorts in sex and head motions (see Table 1). However, we detect a slightly older age (31.17y vs 30.13y) and a lower educational level (11.85y vs 14.23y) in the patient group.
