108:Group  information  guided  ICA  (GIG-ICA)  was  adopted  to  the window-direction concatenated dynamic connectivity patterns to extract the subject-specific independent components (ICs) as inherent networks  with  the  corresponding  weight  coefficients  reflecting  the networks' variability (Du and Fan, 2013; Du et al., 2017). The number of ICs  was  empirically  determined  to  be  20.  We  chose  the  healthy-ICA group to compute the subject-specific ICs at a group level, and then these group-level ICs were used as guidance information to compute ICs of  the  remaining  participants  (FEDN-MDD,  healthy-comparison  individuals, and healthy-base controls) using GIG-ICA. Finally, each subject was characterized by 20 inherent networks and time-varying weight coefficients.
128:In this study, we specifically estimated the 5th, 50th (median), and 95th percentiles with bootstrapping confidence intervals to obtain the normative distribution separately. Fig. S1 (see supplementary materials) shows some estimated percentile curves for a representative dynamic feature, and most individuals were deemed to reside in the normative range with 95 % confidence intervals. These individuals were within the normative range of variation for healthy-base adults of the same age and sex, labeled as normal .  In  addition, those individuals were positioned significantly  exceeding  the  normative  range  labeled  as supra-normal , below  the  normative  range  labeled  as infra-normal .  To  quantify  the extent  of  deviation  in  supra-normal  and  infra-normal,  we  defined  z scores as follows:
134:To assess generalization, we applied 10-fold cross-validation to build an aggregation normative model (Zabihi et al., 2019). We repeatedly trained the polynomial regression model on 90 % of the 289 healthybase  participants  at  different  percentiles,  withholding  the  remaining 10  %  for  estimating  generalization  performance.  This  10-fold  crossvalidation  procedure  yielded  10  normative  models.  Each  of  these models generated a normative distribution based on quantile regressions with 95 % confidence intervals.
136:Having the aggregation normative model based on age and dynamic (network  fluctuation  characteristic  and  FC-dynamic  characteristic) characteristics,  we  next  positioned  each  FEDN-MDD  patient  and healthy-comparison participant within the normative range for healthybase adults of the same age and sex. Thus, each FEDN-MDD patient and the healthy-comparison participant was given 10 individual z scores. We adopted a certainty measure to evaluate the deviation reliability for each subject based on the 10-fold cross-validation normative model. Particularly, the certainty was measured by nz/n, where nz  is the number of nonzeroes  in  these  10 z scores,  and  n  is  the  total  number  of  crossvalidation models (here n = 10). A higher value indicated higher deviation reliability, and vice versa .  Subjects with higher certainty ( ≥ 0.8) were identified as infra- or supra-normal, and their robust z was the average of the nonzeroes z scores.

